%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C7A744CC] ## How does a phone know its orientation?
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:MainMenu.m)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  ![](text:image:4967)
%[text] A cell phone contains three devices:
%[text] - a **magnetometer** that detects the Earth's magnetic field
%[text] - an **accelerometer** that detects linear acceleration
%[text] - a **gyroscope** that detects rotational acceleration \
%[text] Alone, each sensor does not provide enough information to determine the absolute orientation of the phone. But combine these signals with vector mathematics (in a process known as *sensor fusion*), and you can estimate the absolute orientation. 
%[text] In this set of live scripts, you'll learn about vectors and how you can use them to estimate a cell phone's orientation from its sensor readings.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:T_88BC1FDF] # Vector Basics
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] ![](text:image:2301) **Pro-tip**: This live script is meant to be used with the code hidden. Select the `VIEW` tab and switch to `Hide Code`. Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:7b4e) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![](text:image:28ec) **Note:** Your answers should be accurate to at least 3 decimal places. If they are not, they will be marked incorrect.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_3C37332E] ## 1. Vector definition
%[text] A **scalar** quantity is defined by a single numeric value and has no direction. A **vector** quantity, on the other hand, has both **magnitude** and **direction** and is typically defined in terms of several components.
%[text] ![](text:image:951b) **Exercise.** Several physical quantities are described below. Which of them are vector quantities?
%[text]     1\. Your age
%[text]     2\. The velocity of an airplane ![](text:image:8a20)
%[text]     3\. The magnetic force that directs the needle of a compass north ![](text:image:5be7)
%[text]     4\. The temperature on the surface of the sun: 5500°C![](text:image:54d6)
%[text] Type a list of which answers are vector quantities in the space below (for example: 1, 3, 4).
vectorQuantities = [2, 3]; %[control:editfield:7dda]{"position":[21,25]}
GradeVectorQuantities(vectorQuantities)
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C30B7E9D] ## **2. Vector visualization**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_35506087] ### 2D vectors
%[text] A two-dimensional vector contains two scalar components. 
%[text] $\\mathbf{v} = \\left\[ v\_1, v\_2\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \left\[ v\_1, v\_2\]"}
%[text] When graphing vectors, the components are visualized as the $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} extension of the vector: $\\mathbf{v} = \[v\_x, v\_y\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[v\_x, v\_y\]"}. The components determine how far the vector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} extends in the direction of the $x${"altText":"x"} and $y${"altText":"y"} axes.
% Define the vector
v = [2,2]; % default: v=[2,-4] %[control:slider:1392]{"position":[6,7]} %[control:slider:6e11]{"position":[8,9]}

% Plot the vector
figure("position",[1 1 350 350])
hold on
Setup2DVectorPlot([-5 5 -5 5])
PlotVector2DWithComponents(v,"v",1)
hold off
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_45F760C6] ![](text:image:836d) **Try**
%[text] - Use the sliders to set $\\mathbf{v} = \[-3,-3\]\n${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[-3,-3\] "} and then $\\mathbf{v} = \[-4,2\]\n${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[-4,2\] "}, and observe the changes to the vector.
%[text] - Set $\\mathbf{v} = \[2,2\]\n${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[2,2\] "}, how *long* is $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"}? Type your prediction below, and you can check it later. \
vLength = 3; % Default: 0 %[control:editfield:9ba9]{"position":[11,12]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E71DA69C] ### 3D vectors
%[text] A 3D vector has three components
%[text] $\\mathbf{v} = \\left\[ v\_x, v\_y, v\_z\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \left\[ v\_x, v\_y, v\_z\]"}
%[text] Let's graph a 3D vector.
% Define the vector
v = [2,0,2]; % default: v=[2,-4,4] %[control:slider:8d98]{"position":[6,7]} %[control:slider:4776]{"position":[8,9]} %[control:slider:14a3]{"position":[10,11]}

% Plot the vector
axisLims = [-5 5];
figure("position",[1 1 400 400])
hold on
PlotVector3DAxis([axisLims axisLims axisLims])
PlotVector3DWithComponents(v,"v",1)
hold off
view([110 15])
%[text] ![](text:image:5dd3) **Pro-tip**: You can click and drag the 3D plot to rotate the axes.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_45F760C6] ![](text:image:3c72) **Try**
%[text] - Use the sliders to set $\\mathbf{v} = \[3,-4,0\]\n${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[3,-4,0\] "}. What plane does the $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} lie in?
%[text] - Set $\\mathbf{v} = \[2,0,2\]\n${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[2,0,2\] "}. What angle do you think $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} make with the $x${"altText":"x"}-axis? Write your answer below in degrees.  \
axisAngle = 45; %[control:editfield:1421]{"position":[13,15]}
GradeAxisAngle(axisAngle);
%[text] ![](text:image:5cad) **Reflect**
%[text] - Do 4D vectors exist? If so, why aren't they visualized here? \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_B812C5A4] ## 3. Vector magnitude
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EA42348E] ### 2D vectors
%[text] The length of the vector is referred to as it’s **magnitude**. The magnitude is typically denoted using two vertical lines: $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert${"altText":"\Vert \mathbf{v} \Vert"}. In two dimensions, you can compute the magnitude using the Pythagorean theorem.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![The length of a vector ||v|| is the length of the hypotenuse of the triangle with legs v\_x and v\_y in the x- and y- directions, respectively](text:image:1e7f)
%[text] So $\\Vert \\mathbf{v} \\Vert= \\sqrt{ {v\_x}^2 + {v\_y}^2 }${"altText":"\Vert \mathbf{v} \Vert= \sqrt{ {v\_x}^2 + {v\_y}^2 }"}.
%[text] ![](text:image:86c3) **Exercise.** Compute the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{v} = \[3, 4\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[3, 4\]"} and enter the answer below.
% Type the magnitude of v
CheckMag(5); % default: 0 %[control:editfield:5db6]{"position":[10,11]}
%[text] ![](text:image:3a9f) **Reflect**
%[text] - Add the components of $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"}. Is the answer equal to the magnitude? \
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:7f16) **Exercise.** Fill in the expression for magnitude below using MATLAB functions and then observe the plot. 
%[text] ![](text:image:59c8) **Pro-tip.** Access the elements of $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} using indexing as `v(1)` and `v(2)`.  Use `^2` to compute the square and [sqrt](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.sqrt.html) to compute the square root.
% Define the vector
v = [2,2]; % default: v=[2,3] %[control:slider:3139]{"position":[6,7]} %[control:slider:4500]{"position":[8,9]}

% Replace with your expression for the magnitude
mag_a = sqrt(v(1)^2 + v(2)^2); % default NaN %[control:editfield:0f4d]{"position":[9,30]}
% Plot the vector
figure("position",[1 1 350 350])
hold on
Setup2DVectorPlot([-5 5 -5 5])
PlotVector2D([0 0],v,"\Vert \mathbf{v} \Vert \approx" + num2str(mag_a,3),-3)
hold off
%[text] ![](text:image:3330) **Try**
%[text] - Set $\\mathbf{v} = \[2,2\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[2,2\]"}. At the start of the lesson, you thought that the length of this $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} was: \
vLength
%[text]             were you right?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F34E9D1A] ### 3D vectors
%[text] Consider the plot of a 3-dimensional vector $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} and its components.
  %[control:button:0c43]{"position":[1,2]}
v = [4 5 5];
% Plot the vector
axisLims = [-6.5 6.5];
figure("position",[1 1 400 400])
hold on
PlotVector3DAxis([axisLims axisLims axisLims])
PlotVector3D([0 0 0],v,"\mathbf{v}",-1)
PlotVector3D([0 0 0],[v(1) 0 0],"\mathbf{v}_x",-2)
PlotVector3D([v(1) 0 0],[0 v(2) 0],"\mathbf{v}_y",-2)
PlotVector3D([0 0 0],[v(1) v(2) 0],"\mathbf{v}_{x,y}",-2)
PlotVector3D([v(1) v(2) 0],[0 0 v(3)],"\mathbf{v}_{z}",1)
hold off
view([110 25])
%[text] A single application of the Pythagorean theorem is insufficient to compute $||\\mathbf{v}||${"altText":"||\mathbf{v}||"}. 
%%
%[text] ![](text:image:47a2) **Exercise.** Derive the formula for $||\\mathbf{v}||${"altText":"||\mathbf{v}||"} in 3D. Enter the expression below using the symbolic MATLAB variables `v_x`, `v_y`, and `v_z`. 
%[text] **Solution.**
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![decompose a vector in 3D into its planar and coordinate axis components](text:image:16a9)
%[text] Using the triangle in the $x${"altText":"x"}-$y${"altText":"y"} plane, the vector labeled $\\mathbf{v}\_{x,y}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}\_{x,y}"} has magnitude:
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $|| \\mathbf{v}\_{x,y} || = \\sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2}${"altText":"|| \mathbf{v}\_{x,y} || = \sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2}"}
%[text] Using the right triangle with sides $\\mathbf{v}\_{x,y}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}\_{x,y}"} and $v\_z}${"altText":"v\_z}"}, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}${"altText":"\mathbf{v}"} is
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $||\\mathbf{v}|| = \\sqrt{ ||\\mathbf{v}\_{x,y}||^2 + v\_z^2} = \\sqrt{ (\\sqrt{v\_x^2 + v\_y^2})^2 + v\_z^2}${"altText":"||\mathbf{v}|| = \sqrt{ ||\mathbf{v}\_{x,y}||^2 + v\_z^2} = \sqrt{ (\sqrt{v\_x^2 + v\_y^2})^2 + v\_z^2}"}
%[text] So
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $||\\mathbf{v}|| =\\sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2 + v\_z^2}${"altText":"||\mathbf{v}|| =\sqrt{ v\_x^2 + v\_y^2 + v\_z^2}"}
%[text] ![](text:image:4b9d) **Pro-tip.** Symbolic math in MATLAB uses many of the same operators as standard MATLAB: `^2` is used to compute the square and `sqrt()` is used to compute the square root. To learn more about the Symbolic Math Toolbox, you can visit the documentation [here](https://www.mathworks.com/help/symbolic/create-symbolic-numbers-variables-and-expressions.html).
syms v_x v_y v_z % Define symbolic variables
magv = sqrt(v_x^2 + v_y^2 + v_z^2) %[control:editfield:2c60]{"position":[8,35]}
CheckSymbolicMag(magv)
%[text] **Hint:** apply the Pythagorean theorem twice.
%[text] ![](text:image:3e4f) **Exercise.** Compute the magnitude of the vector $\\mathbf{u} = \[3, 5, -3\]${"altText":"\mathbf{u} = \[3, 5, -3\]"} and type the answer below.
% Type the magnitude of u (must match to at least 3 sig figs)
Check3DMag(6.557) %[control:editfield:3df1]{"position":[12,17]}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DCB6223A] ## 4. Vector direction
%[text] Vectors have direction and magnitude. In general, the **direction** of the vector is described by the angle it makes relative to the reference direction. In 2D, you can use the inverse tangent function to compute the angle a vector makes with the $x${"altText":"x"}-axis.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  ![Decompose a vector v into a component v\_x in the x direction and v\_y in the y direction](text:image:9bb3)
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left( \\frac{v\_y}{v\_x} \\right)${"altText":"\theta = \tan^{-1}\left( \frac{v\_y}{v\_x} \right)"}
%[text] ![](text:image:9b80) **Exercise.** Compute the angle, $\\theta${"altText":"\theta"}, that $\\mathbf{v} = \[2, -3\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[2, -3\]"} makes with the $x${"altText":"x"}-axis in degrees (include at least 4 decimal places).
%[text] ![](text:image:3a4d) **Pro-tip.** Use the [atand](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/double.atand.html) function to compute the inverse tangent in degrees (`atan` computes it in radians).
theta = atand(-3/2) %[control:editfield:661a]{"position":[9,20]}
CheckAngle2D(theta)
%[text] The graph below illustrates the angle that a 2D vector makes with the $+x${"altText":"+x"}-axis. 
% Define the vector
v = [-2,2]; % default: v=[2,3] %[control:slider:46b4]{"position":[6,8]} %[control:slider:1c00]{"position":[9,10]}

% Plot the vector
figure("position",[1 1 350 350])
hold on
Setup2DVectorPlot([-5 5 -5 5])
PlotVectorWithAngle([0 0],v) 
hold off
%[text] ![](text:image:93ef) **Try**
%[text] - Set $\\mathbf{v} = \[-3,-3\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[-3,-3\]"} and note the angle. Then set $\\mathbf{v} = \[-1,-1\]${"altText":"\mathbf{v} = \[-1,-1\]"}. Does the orientation change? Does the magnitude change? \
%%
